!                           determine if we allow order split. This is
!                           part of the fix for Mill Direct orders. Don't
!                           want them to split.
!  8.412  10/02/26 j4878    Malvern UPS API Error Tracking Project Add code to get PID from oerx02 for oepack to pass on to
!                           OrderCreate call in WBXMLC
!  8.413  10/07/26 j5724    Reverse edits 8.410 and 8.41 wich would Use
!                           LOC'MILL'DIR and LOC'SHIP'COMP to determine
!                           if we allow order split. This would not allow
!                           Mill Direct orders to split.
! !!COMMENT-MARKER!!LANGUAGE=BASIC!!    DO NOT REMOVE  DO NOT REMOVE
!!
!!NOTE: FIX OC2 RE: STX?
!!NOTE: What to do if billing trashed by actual, then user aborts?  Do we
!!      force PICK'NEED ... and thereby loose the data entered ... or?
!!      more esoteric, save original box records, and re-write if abort?
!!
!!
CIF COMMENTS AND NOT COMMENTS THEN
        This program ended up handling some very weird stuff as a result
        of not requiring the number of boxes to match at any point between
        picking and invoices.  Essentially, the freight computed during
        picking is stored as a constant (HDR'VIA'SUM(2)) and allocated as
        any other constant over the actual boxes.  This allows us to have
        the numbers UPS needs:  the value (defined as BOX'SHP'TOTAL - and
        never includes discounts); the COD amount (all of the money including
        freight, discounts, charges, and tax allocated over the number of
        actual boxes); and freight charges (from the BXV records).
        NOTE:  there is no meaning nor relationship between freight charged
        the customer and freight charged by UPS - and any attempt to add
        these numbers or compare them or whatever is meaningless.

Net Packing Percent:  modifies the percentage weight of a COD versus net
order - with the object being to increase the weight a little on COD orders
so that we reduce the number of tags.  An entry of zero or 100 has no effect.

Example:  Packing weight is 70.00 lbs and Net Packing Percent is 90%
then:  70./.9 = weight we will pack CODs to = 77.78 lbs.


   There is now an overriding customer maximum weight in case the customer
   desires light boxes ... an environment variable OEPACK_HARSH will abort the
   program if a case weight exceeds the customer weight (not recommended).

Note - an overriding max weight can be set on the ship via master ...
if zero it uses the control record.  If zero and other manifest it will use
infinite weight.

Overflow Allowance Percent:  there are two maximum weights to consider ...
the absolute maximum set in "maximum weight allowed" and the "pack weight" set
by type (both in Units Parameters).  Generally the absolute maximum is
around 70lbs and various types pack to a lesser number - in
this example we will use 50lbs.  The overflow allowance is used to determine
how much over the lower number we are allowed to go (without exceeding the
absolute max) when we are trying to get a "whole" item into a box.  A whole
item is either a piece or dozen ... so if we normally pack to 50lbs - BUT we
have 1 dozen left over, should we stuff it into the last box?  The rule is,
take the 50lbs and divide by overflow percent.  Say our percent is 80% then,
50./.80 = 62.50lbs - and if adding that dozen comes to less than 62.50 lbs,
then we will add it ... Most systems are set at 80%, if they are packing too
heavy, increase to 90%.  An entry of zero or 100 will make this do nada.
Further, when packing individual pieces (not whole items) then it uses a
complicated weighting system that works as such:  with few items packed the
62.50 number is used, with lots packed the 50.00 number is used - it is
a sliding scale between the two.

ENDCIF
!!
